/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/_codegen_logs/run_20260618_123649_seed385645705_resume/5__6.py.jsonl:16:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed385645705/5/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"e8686506\" lines=\"172-185\">\n<description>\nAssemble scattered colored fragments into a left-right symmetric rectangle, jigsaw-style.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color; remaining colors form small sprite fragments.\n- Optional roles: 4 singleton pixels of one color mark corners; horizontal bars mark top/bottom edges; paired vertical lines mark left/right edges.\n\nRule:\n- Collect all non-bg connected components as jigsaw pieces.\n- Determine output rectangle dimensions from total piece area.\n- Pack pieces into the rectangle so it is exactly mirror-symmetric (left-right) and same-color pieces never 4-touch.\n\nKey insight: Left-right symmetry plus the no-same-color-touching constraint make the assembly unique; corner/edge markers anchor it when present.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"17829a00\" lines=\"21-36\">\n<description>\nMove each colored object vertically until it touches the matching top or bottom border bar.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- The top row is a full-width bar in one foreground color and the bottom row is a full-width bar in another.\n- All other foreground pixels are 8-connected monochrome objects whose color matches one of those bars.\n\nRule:\n- Top-bar-colored objects move upward so their bbox top is row 1.\n- Bottom-bar-colored objects move downward so their bbox bottom is the row above the bottom bar.\n- A downward-moving one-column segment whose landing overlaps its source becomes the continuous vertical segment swept between them.\n\nKey insight:\nThe horizontal bars are destination anchors: color determines which border an object slides toward, while columns and object shape are otherwise preserved.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"97d7923e\" lines=\"81-94\">\n<description>\nRecolor vertical bars by legend-driven rank selection.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Top-left: monochrome blocks touching row 0; each has color K and height k.\n- Main grid: vertical bars capped top and bottom by a single color, with solid-color interiors.\n\nRule:\n- For each legend entry (K, k), find vertical bars capped by K.\n- Rank bars by interior length (longest = rank 1).\n- Recolor the rank-k bar's interior to K.\n\nKey insight: Legend height is a rank index into length-sorted bars, not a literal size.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"16de56c4\" lines=\"9-34\">\n<description>\nFill periodic progressions that shoot from a unique grid edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.  Sparse non-bg dots of several colors.\n- All repeating colors originate from the same grid edge (top, bottom,\n  left, or right — the one with the most non-bg dots).\n- Each repeating color has exactly 2 dots per shooting-direction line;\n  their gap defines the step.\n- Every non-bg dot lies on a shooting-direction line that contains a\n  repeating color.\n\nRule:\n- Determine the unique shooting edge; left/right → rows, top/bottom → columns.\n- For each shooting-direction line, the repeating color has exactly 2 dots;\n  step = gap between them, offset = first position mod step.\n- A different-colored dot aligned to the same beat (pos % step == offset) is\n  a companion: it absorbs the ray and becomes the fill color, limiting the\n  fill range to the span between repeating and companion dots.\n- An unaligned dot is a bystander and left untouched.\n- Without a companion the repeating color fills the entire line.\n\nKey insight: the shooting edge is identified by counting boundary dots;\nthe gap between the two marker dots is the period; a companion sharing the\nmodular class acts as a wall that stops the ray and dictates the paint color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"a6953f00\" lines=\"45-63\">\n<description>\nExtract the top-left or top-right 2x2 corner crop selected by the top edge clue.\n\nInput structure\n- The input is a square color grid whose answer is one of the two 2x2 crops\n  touching the top edge and a grid corner.\n- A repeated color on the top row, when present, acts as the primary side cue.\n- Otherwise, the two top corner colors have different whole-grid frequencies.\n\nRule\n- If a top-row color repeats, find the repeated top-row occurrence nearest to\n  a left or right edge and take the 2x2 top-corner crop on that side.\n- If the top row has no repeated color, compare the whole-grid frequencies of\n  the two top-corner colors and take the side whose corner color is rarer.\n\nKey insight\n- The task is not asking for an arbitrary 2x2 search: the decisive information\n  is on the top edge, and it selects which top corner crop to keep.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"84ba50d3\" lines=\"36-54\">\n<description>\nDrop the blue sprites downward relative to the red divider, with vertical sprites passing through to the bottom.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- One non-background color forms a single full-width horizontal divider row.\n- The other non-background color forms 4-connected sprites above the divider.\n\nRule:\n- Clear the original sprite pixels.\n- A one-column sprite falls to the bottom of its column and deletes the divider\n  pixel it crossed.\n- Any wider sprite falls until its unique widest horizontal row is immediately\n  above the divider row; if the moved sprite overlaps the divider, it covers it.\n\nKey insight:\nThe red row is not a generic floor: only one-column sprites pass through to the\nbottom, while wider sprites are registered by their widest horizontal segment.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6ca952ad\" lines=\"15-30\">\n<description>\nDrop every large foreground object straight down to the bottom while leaving small marker objects fixed.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has a dominant background color and exactly one foreground color.\n- The foreground is split into separated 4-connected objects.\n- Objects of area at most three are stationary markers; larger objects are moving sprites.\n\nRule:\n- Clear each moving sprite from its original position.\n- Translate each moving sprite vertically downward until its bbox bottom reaches the grid bottom.\n- Repaint each translated sprite in its original color; small markers stay unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- Object area separates the active falling sprites from the small fixed markers, and all active sprites follow the same vertical path.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"b74ca5d1\" lines=\"157-172\">\n<description>\nSwap each 5x5 sprite with its corner marker color and stamp same-marker composite masks into the matching corner.\n\nInput structure:\n- A 30x30 grid has one dominant background color.\n- Non-background corner pixels are marker labels.\n- Every other foreground color is the body of one 5x5 sprite containing exactly one pixel of a corner marker color.\n\nRule:\n- For each sprite, recolor body pixels to the sprite marker color and recolor the marker pixel back to its body color.\n- If a marker lies on the side facing its matching corner and another same-marker sprite has a body pixel at that same local cell, use that visible body color for the marker pixel.\n- For each corner marker, overlay the masks of all sprites with that marker color into the corner 5x5 block and paint the visible composite with the marker color.\n\nKey insight:\nThe corner pixels are labels that group 5x5 sprites by their embedded marker color; each labeled corner shows the grouped sprite silhouette.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"bc93ec48\" lines=\"48-63\">\n<description>\nCopy each foreground object anchored in a true grid corner clockwise into the next corner.\n\nInput structure\n- The dominant color is the background.\n- Foreground objects are monochromatic 4-connected components.\n- Relevant objects are exactly those whose bounding box touches two adjacent borders at one grid corner.\n\nRule\n- Start from the input unchanged.\n- For each corner object, copy its pixel mask and color to the next corner clockwise: top-left to top-right, top-right to bottom-right, bottom-right to bottom-left, and bottom-left to top-left.\n- Preserve each object's orientation by aligning its bounding box to the destination corner; stamps may overwrite existing pixels at the destination, but source pixels are not cleared.\n\nKey insight\n- Interior and side-only objects are distractors; only components actually anchored to a corner participate in the clockwise perimeter copy.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"a416fc5b\" lines=\"95-106\">\n<description>\nAdd the two missing colored arms of a centered diamond-sprite motif, or clear a completed motif to a blank square.\n\nInput structure\nAn 11x11 board has orange background and pink separator bars forming nine 3x3 cells. Occupied cells contain a four-pixel diamond sprite; the center cell and one neighboring cell are red, and a completed board may also contain one grey and one teal sprite.\n\nRule\nWith only the two red sprites present, preserve the board and add grey and teal diamond sprites 135 degrees clockwise and counterclockwise from the non-center red arm. If those expected grey and teal sprites are already present, output an all-background square whose side length is the total number of sprite pixels.\n\nKey insight\nThe non-center red sprite is an eight-neighbor direction from the center; the other two colored arms are fixed rotations of that direction.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6ffe8f07\" lines=\"49-64\">\n<description>\nCast yellow horizontal and vertical rays from the teal rectangle until blue blockers stop them.\n\nInput structure:\n- A black-background grid containing one solid teal rectangle.\n- Blue objects are blockers; other non-background objects may lie in the ray paths.\n- Yellow is absent from the input and is the output paint color.\n\nRule:\n- From every row of the teal rectangle, cast rays left and right to the grid edge.\n- From every column of the teal rectangle, cast rays up and down to the grid edge.\n- A ray stops before a blue pixel; all crossed non-blue, non-teal pixels become yellow.\n\nKey insight:\nThe teal rectangle defines a full cross-shaped projection, while blue objects are walls and red/background cells are simply paintable space.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e734a0e8\" lines=\"44-59\">\n<description>\nCopy the unique colored sprite tile into every tiled cell marked by an internal separator-colored pixel.\n\nInput structure\n- Full same-color separator rows and columns partition the grid into equal rectangular tiles.\n- One tile contains the only non-background, non-separator colored sprite.\n- Target tiles are otherwise background-colored but contain at least one internal pixel of the separator color.\n\nRule\n- Keep the separator lines and the source sprite tile unchanged.\n- Replace each marked target tile with an exact copy of the source tile.\n- Unmarked background-only tiles remain unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The internal separator-colored pixels are placement markers; the single colored tile is the template to stamp into those marked cells.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"8dab14c2\" lines=\"120-133\">\n<description>\nComplete each boundary tab or notch by adding the complementary feature on the opposite side of the foreground object.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has exactly two colors: a dominant background and one foreground object.\n- The foreground object is a thick rectilinear shape with one-pixel tabs and notches on its boundary.\n\nRule\n- A foreground tab is a one-neighbor boundary pixel; project through its full row or column span and remove the opposite edge pixel to make a matching notch.\n- A background notch is a one-pixel cut with three foreground 4-neighbors; project across the full row or column span and add a tab just outside the opposite edge.\n- Notches immediately beside a tab source are not independent sources, because that gap belongs to the tab's local boundary shape.\n\nKey insight\n- The changed pixels are not color-specific noise; they are paired jigsaw-like boundary features on opposite sides of the same thick row or column cross-section.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
